WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.940
When is a synapse finished?

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:09.080
When it is there, when it releases, or when the other side responds?

3
00:00:10.183 --> 00:00:16.363
A mature muscle fiber is usually contacted by one motor axon, at one site, using one

4
00:00:16.363 --> 00:00:17.363
transmitter.

5
00:00:17.917 --> 00:00:20.937
A neuron in the brain receives thousands of synapses.

6
00:00:21.937 --> 00:00:27.197
Excitatory and inhibitory synapses share one dendrite, so each contact must specify

7
00:00:27.197 --> 00:00:28.197
its kind.

8
00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:30.690
A finished synapse does three things.

9
00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:34.030
It is there. It releases transmitter.

10
00:00:34.990 --> 00:00:36.170
And the other side responds.

11
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:40.080
They can come apart, and each needs its own measurement.

12
00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:42.873
A microscope shows it is there.

13
00:00:43.733 --> 00:00:46.473
A dye taken up by recycling vesicles shows release.

14
00:00:47.393 --> 00:00:48.873
A recording shows the response.

15
00:00:53.317 --> 00:00:57.077
Take stargazer, a mouse that tosses its head upward and has seizures.

16
00:00:58.217 --> 00:01:02.477
In its cerebellum, granule cells receive synapses from mossy fibers.

17
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:07.020
AMPA receptors carry most fast excitatory current.

18
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.420
NMDA receptors open when glutamate and a co-agonist are bound.

19
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:16.820
Near rest, magnesium from outside blocks most of their current.

20
00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:25.920
In stargazer, these synapses give NMDA responses but no AMPA responses.

21
00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:28.320
Predict: which property failed?

22
00:01:34.783 --> 00:01:37.983
Not being there. The synapses are present.

23
00:01:38.967 --> 00:01:40.227
Not release either.

24
00:01:40.907 --> 00:01:45.167
An NMDA response needs glutamate, so transmitter is being released.

25
00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:48.130
What failed is one kind of response.

26
00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:52.330
The same synapse responds through one receptor and not the other.

27
00:01:56.750 --> 00:02:00.130
The gene encodes stargazin, a small membrane protein.

28
00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:03.610
It brings AMPA receptors to the cell surface.

29
00:02:04.633 --> 00:02:10.573
Its tail binds PSD-95, a scaffold under the synapse, and that holds the receptors

30
00:02:10.573 --> 00:02:11.573
there.

31
00:02:11.966 --> 00:02:13.526
A count would miss this defect.

32
00:02:14.366 --> 00:02:17.146
A recording shows it, and so does labeling the receptors.

33
00:02:18.366 --> 00:02:20.466
So carry one idea through this lesson.

34
00:02:21.366 --> 00:02:24.086
A synapse that is there is not yet a synapse that works.

35
00:02:25.116 --> 00:02:28.616
Takeaway: each experiment sees only the properties it measures.
